.TH I86 1 "November 19, 2026"
.SH NAME
i86 \- run DOS on an emulated 8086, 80186 or 80286
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B i86
[
.B \-dGgqrTv
] [
.B \-c
.I cpu
] [
.B \-m
.I kbytes
] [
.B \-e
.I kbytes
] [
.B \-f
.I frames
] [
.B \-S
.I mhz
] [
.B \-w
.I columns
] [
.B \-s
.I pagefile
] [
.B \-k
.I keys
] [
.B \-a
.I floppy
] [
.B \-b
.I floppy
] [
.B \-h
.I disk
] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I I86
is a personal computer in a process: an Intel 8086, 80186 or 80286 in real
mode, up to 640 kilobytes of memory, a color text display, a keyboard, an
8254 timer that interrupts 18.2 times a second until the guest programs
another rate, a clock and up to two floppy and two hard disks held in
image files.
It starts the boot sector of the first floppy image, or of the first hard
disk image when no floppy is given, as a PC starts DOS.
.PP
The guest's memory is larger than the process that holds it.
.I I86
keeps
.I frames
pages of one kilobyte resident and holds the others in
.IR pagefile ,
which it creates on the first page it must put aside.
The name is removed as soon as the file is open, so the pages occupy
space that no directory shows and that the system frees when the
emulator ends, however it ends.
A
.I pagefile
that already exists is not touched and ends the session with status 3.
A page the guest never wrote, or filled with zeros, occupies no file space.
A guest whose working set fits the frames runs without file traffic;
one that does not runs at the speed of the device under
.IR pagefile .
.PP
The display is the terminal.
Text the guest writes, through the firmware or straight into display
memory, is drawn with cursor addressing and the eight colors in two
intensities; characters outside ASCII are drawn as their nearest ASCII
shape.
The terminal needs 25 lines and the guest's width in columns, so on the
PicoCalc panel's 53 columns run with
.B \-w
.B 40
or with
.BR \-g .
Control-] opens a one-line menu:
.B q
leaves the emulator,
.B a
or
.B b
changes the image in that floppy drive while the guest runs, and any other
key returns.
The images offered are the files named
.I *.img
and
.I *.ima
in the directory of the drive's current image; a name typed without a
slash is taken from that directory.
DOS reads a changed disk's directory afresh on its next access after a
pause of two seconds.
With
.B \-q
or
.B \-d
Control-] leaves at once.
.PP
COM1 has a Microsoft serial mouse on it, which DOS mouse drivers such as
CTMOUSE find by themselves.
Control-\e turns mouse keys on and off; while they are on, the panel
shows a white arrow in its top margin, the arrow keys move the mouse,
faster while a key is held,
.B [
and
.B ]
click the left and right button, and
.B {
and
.B }
press that button and hold it until either of its keys comes again.
All other keys type as usual.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BI \-a " floppy"
.TP
.BI \-b " floppy"
The images of drives A and B: 160, 180, 320 or 360 kilobytes, 720
kilobytes, 1.2, 1.44 or 2.88 megabytes.
.TP
.BI \-c " cpu"
The processor:
.BR 8086 ,
.BR 8088 ,
.B 186
or
.B 286
(the default).
The 8086 and 8088 run the same instructions and differ only in the pace of
.BR \-S .
.TP
.B \-d
Draw nothing while running and print the 25 lines of the display on exit.
.TP
.BI \-e " kbytes"
Expanded memory, 16 to 32768 kilobytes in steps of 16, under the Lotus,
Intel and Microsoft Expanded Memory Specification 4.0 through interrupt
67h.
DOS finds it through the driver
.IR EMS.SYS ,
loaded by the line
.B DEVICE=C:\eEMS.SYS
in
.IR CONFIG.SYS ;
the driver stays resident only when
.B \-e
is given.
Its pages take no memory until written and are kept in the page file.
Windows 3.0 in real mode banks applications into it.
.TP
.BI \-f " frames"
The number of resident pages, 119 by default, which the RP2040 process
window holds with
.B \-e
up to 2048; a larger
.B \-e
needs fewer.
.TP
.B \-g
Use the PicoCalc's panel,
.IR /dev/lcdgfx ,
as the display and the terminal for the keys alone.
Both text widths fill the panel, 80 columns in characters four pixels
wide, with all of code page 437; the graphics modes 4, 5 and 6 of the
color adapter are drawn, mode 6 at half its 640 pixels a line.
A graphics mode takes 24 of the
.I frames
while it lasts, eight for its odd scan lines and 16 for a copy of the
lines last sent, so that only the bytes that differ go to the panel;
with fewer to spare it takes eight and sends every written line whole.
A text mode moves the panel's scroll start when the whole screen
scrolls, and draws only the cells that then differ.
.TP
.B \-G
As
.BR \-g ,
without the copy of the lines and without moving the scroll start:
every line or cell the guest writes goes to the panel, for comparison.
The Control-] menu appears on the console, which has the panel for as
long as the menu is open.
Without the device the terminal is the display and
.I i86
says so.
.TP
.BI \-h " disk"
A hard disk image, with its partition table; the first is drive C, a
second is drive D.
The geometry is read from the end of the first partition, or taken as 16
heads of 63 sectors.
.TP
.BI \-k " keys"
Type the characters of
.I keys
whenever the guest waits for the keyboard, and exit when it waits after
the last one.
.TP
.BI \-m " kbytes"
Guest memory, 64 to 640, by default 640.
.TP
.B \-q
Draw nothing.
.TP
.B \-r
Open the images read-only; the guest's writes fail as on a protected disk.
.TP
.BI \-s " pagefile"
The file for pages that are not resident, by default
.IR /tmp/i86. pid
for the emulator's process number.
.TP
.BI \-S " mhz"
Keep the guest to the speed of the chosen processor at this clock, from 1
to 40 MHz, 4.77 for the IBM PC's 8088: the emulator waits whenever the
guest runs ahead.
The pace is that of one loop of everyday instructions, so other programs
run somewhat faster or slower than on the machine.
It only slows the guest; where the host is slower than the clock asked
for, as an RP2040 at 125 MHz is beyond about 4.2 MHz of 8088,
.B \-S
leaves the speed as it was, and
.B \-v
shows the rate reached.
It has no effect with
.BR \-T .
.TP
.B \-T
Replace the host's clock with a count of executed instructions and a
fixed date, so that two runs do the same.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the page statistics and the instructions run a second on exit, and
the rate
.B \-S
aims at.
.TP
.BI \-w " columns"
Start in the 40- or the 80-column text mode (80 by default).
.SH LIMITS
The 80286 has no protected mode: the machine status word reads real mode
and cannot be changed, so programs that need extended memory or a DOS
extender do not run, and Windows 3.0 runs in real mode only.
Graphics modes exist only with
.B \-g
and are those of the color adapter; there is no EGA or VGA.
There is no numeric coprocessor, no sound and no parallel port; COM1
serves only the mouse, which has two buttons.
Every key arrives as a press followed at once by its release, since a
terminal reports no releases: a program that acts while a key is held
sees it only for an instant.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/lcdgfx
the panel, with
.B \-g
.TP
.IR /tmp/i86. pid
the name the default page file is created under
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status 0 when the user or the end of
.I keys
ends the session, 1 when no image boots, 2 for a usage error or an
unusable image, 3 when the page file cannot be written or read back, 4
when the 80186 or 80286 meets an instruction it does not have and the
guest has installed no handler for it (the address is printed), and
128 plus the signal number when an interrupt, hangup or termination signal
ends it.
In every case the terminal mode is restored.
